    text: "[Exam 01 practice key · decrease? · Q1] What is a Recurrent Neural Network (RNN)? How does it differ from a standard feedforward neural network? An RNN is a neural network designed for sequential data. Unlike a feedforward network that processes each input independently, an RNN has a \"hidden state\" that gets updated at each time step, carrying information from previous inputs. This allows it to maintain a form of memory across a sequence, making it suitable for tasks like language modeling where word order matters.",
