    source: "exam01",
    sourceLabel: "Exam 01 practice key",
    text: "[Exam 01 practice key · In self-attention, every token's query is compared to every token's key in a single matrix · Q3] What is \"perplexity\"? If a model has lower perplexity, is it better or worse? Why? Perplexity is e^(cross-entropy loss) -- it measures how \"surprised\" or \"confused\" the model is by the text. Lower perplexity means the model is better, because it means the model assigns higher probability to the correct tokens (less surprised). A perplexity of 1 would mean the model perfectly predicts every next token. A high perplexity means the model is uncertain and often wrong.",
