    text: "[Practice packet 02 key · central · Q9] What is “benchmark contamination,” and why is it a growing problem for LLM evaluation? Contamination is when benchmark test data (questions and/or answers) ends up in a model’s pre-training corpus -- the model can memorize instead of solve. It’s a growing problem because modern pre-training corpora are enormous web scrapes that inevitably include benchmark test sets, answer keys, and discussion threads about them. It makes benchmark scores unreliable as measures of real capability, which is why researchers increasingly prefer held-out private benchmarks and continuously-refreshed ones.",
