    text: "[Practice packet 02 key · comparison? · Q1] Why is LLM inference often more memory-bandwidth-bound than compute-bound, especially at batch size 1? At each decoding step the model has to read all its weights from GPU memory just to produce one token -- for a 7B model in FP16, that’s ~14 GB of reads per step. Memory bandwidth becomes the bottleneck long before the matrix-multiply compute does. This is why bigger batches help: you amortize the weight reads across many parallel sequences, shifting the bottleneck back toward compute.",
